w this has all sorts of shades of grey  when it comes to problematic countries  including Saudi Arabia and Pakistan but  on the aggregate I think it's useful  when you're approaching a country and  trying to figure out whether it's a  threat or an ally to see we're on that  spectrum they fall and if you look at  the type of assistance that Iran is  giving to the Palestinian Islamic Jihad  to Hezbollah  and even the safe harbor that Iran is  giving to al-qaeda and elements of Ansar  al-islam for example the type of  assistance and aid and comfort is  definitely in the latter category  it's definitely assisting international  instability it's not a net positive for  regional security sir talk to me is it  possible that regardless of the regime  in Iran that it will seek a nuclear  capability due to contemporary  realpolitik and its security needs as  well as a result of its historical  experience for example Russia and  Britain in the early 20th century  carving into spheres of influence oh  absolutely I think that the those people  that think that a denuclearized Iran is  a possibility are living on a cloud I  think the Iranian nuclear program is  simply too far advanced to be rolled  back wholesale and not only that but  that sort sort of effort is not going to  meet with support from the Iranian  population even if they're against the  regime so we have to come to grips with  the fact that a nuclear Iran at some  point two years from now three years  from now ten years from now is going to  be a reality and that precisely that  calculation is what makes the discussion  of regime character so important  because let me borrow a phrase from the  NRA it's not guns that kill people it's  people that kill people and I know  everybody laughs but I think that's that  there's actually more than a little bit  of truth to that the type of weapons  that Iran is pursuing the type of  capabilities that Iran is pursuing are a  threat to regional peace and security if  you have a 