guity about what to do with Iran  what element of the Iranian threat and  and in my book I posit that the Iranian  threat is actually much broader than  just the nuclear question it has to do  with support for terrorist organizations  has to do with what they're doing in the  Persian Gulf and why they're doing it  what they're doing in Iraq for example  the administration has tended to take  depending on your perspective on that  debate rather different positions but  the aggregate result has been that  there's just a lot of inaction and the  problem here is that the nuclear clock  is now ticking very quickly and very  loudly and it is high time for the  United States and the US government to  actually become serious about this sort  of question it's we don't longer have  the luxury of long lead times I think a  great administration official once said  to do this sort of thing and when we  begin to talk about what our strategy  should be those types of personnel  issues become very important the  administration does not have in my  estimation a really coherent strategy  towards what it wants Iran to look like  it's very easy to say you support Iran's  urge for democracy it's another thing  entirely to do things that prove it you  discussed quite thoroughly the process  by which Iran might go nuclear this  issue of matching nuclear investment  with a ballistic missile investment into  the pursuing both uranium enrichment and  in reprocessing the level of their  activity I'm wondering at what what kind  of points what kind of signals would  they send to the international community  would they go forward with a testing  program or what they not would that be  clandestine would they announce  themselves as a nuclear weapons state of  what they not will they go forward and  deploy deploy ballistic missiles with  nuclear capability in the absence of  those two measures how would they go  about the process of doing it what do we  know about that no I think it's an  excellent question and I talked a little  bit earlier about timelines and about  you know factors that go into gauging  your timeline I happen to have a third  separate gauge my gauge is the pace of  the ballistic missile program in Iran  because given the uncertainty with their  nuclear development as soon as Iran can  credibly reach a certain point with a  delivery system you have to assume that  that place or that sphere will be hit by  a nuclear capable missile that's just  prudence dictates that and Iran's the  premier missile in Iran strategic  arsenal the shahab-3 already has a  publicly acknowledged distance of 2,000  kilometers which means that parts of  Turkey all of Israel and even parts of  India are within range they've actually  spent a lot of money on investing and  upgrading that the distance and the  accuracy of that missile they're now  introducing solid motor engines solid  fuel engines for the shahab-3 and  they're trying to increase the accuracy  the projected estimation is that over  the next couple of years the shahab-3  will have a 4,000 kilometer range which  would mean that at least half of Europe  will fall under Iran's nuclear threat by  default and this I think is a really  important benchmark we tend to talk  about the nuclear program in isolation  with the other strategic programs that  Iran is pursuing but more than anything  else the degree to which Iran is  matching investments in nuclear  development with investments in delivery  systems belies a  very substantial intention on their part  if you are pursuing a civilian energy  program and I actually have other  reasons to believe that they're not all  talked about in a second but if you're  pursuing civilian energy program you  will not be closely linking your  ballistic missile program with a  civilian energy program you simply won't  do it it's not prudent it's also not for  him to allocate that much that much  resources to have one keep pace with the  other the other reason and it should  probably should have mentioned this  earlier that I don't think that Iran is  pursuing a civilian nuclear energy  program is because we know that Iran is  a substantial oil exporter almost four  million barrels a day and it is a major  part of opening and we know how much  money it's gleaning as a result of  political instability associated with  the war on terror so the question is why  can't they use their oil but that's  actually I don't think a good question  because everybody can say well they want  to diversify what the real benchmark I  think for me is is that Iran exports  almost four million barrels of oil a day  and yet imports almost 1 billion barrels  of refined gasoline annually and this  suggests that they have a big lag  between what they export and the  refining capability if they were  actually interested in sustainable  energy for their population they would  spend the money not on nuclear reactors  but on refineries and refineries as we  know are much cheaper and if they did  that you could actually make a credible  case that you know this is not enough  but the shortfall that you see in the  gasoline production in the availability  of gasoline in Iran suggests that the  oil is seen not as a net benefit for the  population but as a strategic commodity  to be bartered for other benefits like  money that can be used in the nuclear  program we know that before the 1991  Gulf War that the Iraqi nuclear program  was very extensive there were tens of  thousands of nuclear  missions scientists and engineers level  of effort was incredibly high in the  billions close to ten billion dollar  program what do we know about the level  of effort of the Iranian program and to  what extent are they getting for  technical support by now the Iranian  nuclear effort is not really Iranian  it's really an international affair if  you go to Tehran and I've colleagues of  mine who were there recently they say  you know Georgian Russian Ukrainian  they're ubiquitous 