es with the president  what would you tell him he needs to  start thinking about piece of ear well  after I got my stammering out of the way  I would say that that really I mean it's  this two-tier approach that in my  opinion holds the most the most  potential if Iran goes nuclear tomorrow  all of this discussion is besides the  point because all the things that I have  said really flow from it new potential  nuclear arms race less hospitable  Persian Gulf etc etc so the idea is you  have to keep the window of opportunity  open as long as possible but that's the  easy part I know it doesn't sound easy  but that's the easy part  and once we have that window of  opportunity once we've delayed the  Iranian nuclear program because it's  quite clear that we can the Israelis  used to talk about late 2004 as what  they call the point of no return for the  program when they have all the  indigenous capabilities necessary to  build a nuclear weapon  now these rails are talking about 2007  or late 2006 so it's quite clear that  you can flex their timeline what you  can't do is you can't change the  endpoint Iranians whether they're for  the regime or against the regime want  nuclear weapons and want to be a nuclear  power in its own right and want to see  that that great power table so the  question becomes when they do get a seat  at the table because I'm firmly  convinced that they will how do you  structure your outreach so that they the  regime that ultimately takes possession  of those weapons weapons is a mature one  it's not a threat to the United States  and is reasonably pluralistic and that I  think is we haven't talked much about  that but that I think is a number of  initiatives in and of themselves for  example the types of outreach we were  doing to the Soviet Union during the  Cold War are markedly absent today both  with regard to Iran and frankly the rest  of the Middle East  I I did a little bit of number crunching  in part of my research for my book and  found out that or at least the numbers  that I was given was that public  broadcasting towards Iran through the  broadcasting Board of Governors for 2005  was estimated initially at sixteen point  four million dollars and that includes a  rate both radio and television there are  70 million Iranians living in Iran and  so again not a mathematician but roughly  translated out that's slightly less than  22 cents per Iranian per year you can  argue about how much we should increase  that certainly but I frankly think that  by any yardstick this is probably a  small price and it should be increased  it's less than a third of what we were  paying per Soviet in 1983 so we have a  ways to go in terms of our public  outreach but we also need to have a  coherent vision of what we want our on  to look like and frankly time is running  short for us to articulate that vision  and to empower a forces both within Iran  and outside of Iran that can actually  make that vision a reality  pleasure copies of T around rising are  available for purchase out in the  hallway and Ilan has agreed to stay  around and sign copies of the book and  answer your other questions that you may  have please join me in thanking him for  the insightful presentation  [Applause]  ahman vermin is vice president for  policy at the American foreign policy  council and adjunct professor of  international law and global security at  the National Defense University in  Washington DC Tehran Rising is published  by rowman &amp; littlefield on the web at  Roman littlefield calm
 

